/* Chapter 3 — Motivational Interviewing as a Counseling Style. Source: SAMHSA TIP 35 (2019), pp. 35–64. Study content only from the book. */
window.MI_CHAPTERS.push({
  id: 3,
  title: 'Motivational Interviewing as a Counseling Style',
  pages: '35–64',
  quote: { text: 'Motivational interviewing is a person-centered counseling style for addressing the common problem of ambivalence about change.', by: 'Miller & Rollnick, 2013, p. 21' },
  keyMessages: [
    'The spirit of motivational interviewing (MI) is the foundation of the counseling skills required for enhancing clients’ motivation to change.',
    'Ambivalence about change is normal; resolving clients’ ambivalence about substance use is a key MI focus.',
    'Resistance to change is an expression of ambivalence about change, not a client trait or characteristic.',
    'Reflective listening is fundamental to the four MI processes (engaging, focusing, evoking and planning) and core counseling strategies.'
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: 'intro', h: 'Introduction to MI', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI is a counseling style based on these assumptions:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Ambivalence about substance use and change is <strong>normal</strong> and is an important motivational barrier to substance use behavior change.',
        'Ambivalence can be resolved by exploring the client’s <strong>intrinsic motivations and values</strong>.',
        'Your alliance with the client is a <strong>collaborative partnership</strong> to which you each bring important expertise.',
        'An empathic, supportive counseling style provides conditions under which change can occur.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI reduces or eliminates substance use and other health-risk behaviors in many settings and across genders, ages, races and ethnicities. An analysis of <strong>more than 200 randomized clinical trials</strong> found significant efficacy of MI in treating SUDs.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI builds on <strong>Carl Rogers’</strong> humanistic theories about people’s capacity for free choice and self-determination. Rogers identified the sufficient conditions for client change — now called the “common factors” of therapy, including counselor empathy.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Your main goals in MI are to <strong>express empathy</strong> and <strong>elicit clients’ reasons for and commitment to changing</strong> substance use behaviors. MI is particularly helpful in <strong>Precontemplation and Contemplation</strong>, when readiness is low, but it is useful throughout the change cycle.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'spirit', h: 'The Spirit of MI', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Collaborative counselor–client relationships are the essence of MI; <strong>without them, MI techniques are ineffective</strong>. Counselor MI spirit is associated with positive client engagement behaviors (self-disclosure, cooperation) and positive outcomes in health-related behaviors.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Element (PACE)', 'What it means'], rows: [
        ['Partnership', 'Active collaboration between you and the client. The client is more willing to express concerns when you are empathetic and genuinely curious. You are influential, but <strong>the client drives the conversation</strong>.'],
        ['Acceptance', 'Respect for and approval of the client — not agreeing with everything, but showing your intention to understand the client’s point of view. Has four components (below).'],
        ['Compassion', 'Active promotion of the client’s welfare and prioritization of client needs.'],
        ['Evocation', 'Eliciting and exploring the motivations, values, strengths and resources the client <strong>already has</strong>.']
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'The four components of acceptance' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Absolute worth</strong> — prizing the inherent worth and potential of the client.',
        '<strong>Accurate empathy</strong> — active interest in, and effort to understand, the client’s internal perspective, shown by genuine curiosity and reflective listening.',
        '<strong>Autonomy support</strong> — honoring the client’s right to and capacity for self-direction.',
        '<strong>Affirmation</strong> — acknowledging the client’s values and strengths.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Remember the four elements with the acronym <strong>PACE</strong> (Stinson & Clark, 2017). Your specific strategies should emphasize one or more of them.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'person-centered', h: 'Principles of Person-Centered Counseling', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI is linked to Rogers’ theory of the “critical conditions for change”: clients change in a relationship where the counselor is <strong>genuine and warm</strong>, expresses <strong>unconditional positive regard</strong>, and displays <strong>accurate empathy</strong>.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI adds a dimension: it follows person-centered principles <em>but also guides the conversation toward a specific, client-driven change goal</em>. <strong>MI is more directive than purely person-centered counseling.</strong> Its guiding principles:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'SUD treatment services exist to help recipients; the client’s needs take precedence over the counselor’s or organization’s.',
        'The client engages in a process of self-change; you facilitate the natural process of change.',
        'The client is the expert in his or her own life.',
        'You do not make change happen.',
        'People have their own motivation, strengths and resources; counselors help activate them.',
        'You are not responsible for all the good ideas about change, and probably don’t have the best ideas for any particular client.',
        'Change requires a partnership and a “collaboration of expertise.”',
        'You must understand the client’s perspective on his or her problems and need to change.',
        'The relationship is not a power struggle; conversations about change should not become debates.',
        '<strong>Motivation for change is evoked from, not given to, the client.</strong>',
        'People make their own decisions about taking action. <strong>It is not a change goal until the client says so.</strong>',
        'The spirit of MI and client-centered principles foster a sound therapeutic alliance.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Person-centered approaches consistent with MI improved outcomes in AUD — including readiness to change and reduced alcohol use — over several sessions.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'whats-new', h: 'What Is New in MI', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Exhibit 3.1 compares the original (1991, 2002) and updated (2013) versions of MI.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Topic', 'Original version', 'Updated version'], rows: [
        ['Basis of the approach', 'Four principles: (1) <strong>Express empathy</strong> through reflective listening; (2) <strong>Develop discrepancy</strong> between goals/values and current behavior; (3) <strong>Roll with resistance</strong> — don’t argue for change or against the status quo; (4) <strong>Support self-efficacy</strong>.', 'Four processes: (1) <strong>Engaging</strong> — the relational foundation; (2) <strong>Focusing</strong> — identifies agenda and change goals; (3) <strong>Evoking</strong> — core skills and strategies to move toward a specific change goal; (4) <strong>Planning</strong> — the bridge to behavior change.'],
        ['Emphasis', 'The four general principles (still helpful).', 'Evoking change talk and commitment to change are the primary principles. The four processes replace the Phase I and II stages; OARS is integrated into them; the old principles are folded in as reflective listening or strategic responses.'],
        ['Resistance', 'A characteristic of the client.', 'An expression of sustain talk and the status quo side of ambivalence, arising out of counselor–client discord.'],
        ['Response to resistance', 'Rolling with resistance.', 'Strategies to lessen sustain talk and counselor–client discord.'],
        ['Client statements favoring change', 'Self-motivating statements.', 'Change talk.'],
        ['Decisional balancing', 'A strategy to help clients move in one direction, toward changing a behavior.', 'Used to help clients make a decision <strong>without favoring a direction</strong>. May help assess readiness, but may <strong>increase ambivalence</strong> in clients who are contemplating change.']
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 3.2 — Misconceptions and clarifications' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Misconception', 'Clarification'], rows: [
        ['MI is nondirective, Rogerian therapy.', 'MI shares humanistic principles but is not Rogerian therapy: it has clearly identified target behaviors and change goals, differential evoking and strengthening of motivation, and a <strong>strategic component</strong> moving clients toward a specific behavioral goal.'],
        ['MI is a counseling technique.', 'MI is not a technique. It is a <strong>style of being with people</strong> that uses specific clinical skills to foster motivation to change.'],
        ['MI is a “school” of counseling or psychotherapy.', 'Some theories underlie its spirit and style, but it was not meant to be a theory of change with a comprehensive set of clinical skills.'],
        ['MI and the SOC are the same.', 'They developed around the same time but are distinct; neither is an essential part of the other. They are compatible and complementary. MI is also compatible with CBT.'],
        ['MI always uses assessment feedback.', 'Assessment feedback delivered in MI style was an adaptation that became MET. Personalized feedback can help clients low in readiness but is <strong>not a necessary part of MI</strong>.'],
        ['Counselors can motivate clients to change.', 'You cannot manufacture motivation that is not already in clients. MI is a partnership to <strong>discover</strong> their motivation, respecting autonomy and self-determination.']
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'ambivalence', h: 'Ambivalence, Change Talk and Sustain Talk', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Ambivalence — feeling two ways about an important change — is normal. Frequently it, not a lack of knowledge or skills, is the roadblock to change. People with SUDs are often aware of the risks but keep using; <strong>the tension between these feelings is ambivalence</strong>.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'As clients move from Precontemplation to Contemplation, their feelings of conflict increase. The tension may move them toward change, but often it leads them to avoid thinking about the problem. <strong>View ambivalence not as denial or resistance</strong> but as normal — interpreting it as denial evokes discord.' },
      { t: 'p', x: '<strong>Sustain talk</strong>: client statements that support not changing a health-risk behavior. <strong>Change talk</strong>: client statements that favor change. They are the two sides of ambivalence. Clients stuck in ambivalence produce a lot of sustain talk; clients more ready to change produce more, and stronger, change talk.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'More sustain talk in sessions → <strong>poorer</strong> substance use outcomes.',
        'MI-consistent counselor behavior that elicits and reflects change talk, more change talk than sustain talk, and stronger commitment change talk → <strong>better</strong> outcomes.',
        'Counselor empathy is linked to eliciting change talk.'
      ] },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Your main goal', x: 'In MI, your main goal is to <strong>evoke change talk and minimize evoking or reinforcing sustain talk</strong> in counseling sessions.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'DARN-CAT: kinds of change talk' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Type', 'Expressed as', 'Example'], rows: [
        ['Desire', 'Wanting something different', '“I want to find an AA meeting.” / “I hope to start going to AA.”'],
        ['Ability', 'Self-perception of capability', '“I could start going to AA.”'],
        ['Reasons', 'Arguments for change', '“Going to AA would help me feel more supported.”'],
        ['Need', 'Importance or urgency', '“I have to stop drinking.”'],
        ['Commitment', 'A promise to change', '“I swear I will go to an AA meeting this year.”'],
        ['Activation', 'Movement toward action', '“I’m ready to go to my first AA meeting.”'],
        ['Taking steps', 'Something already done to change', '“I went to an AA meeting.” / “I avoided a party where friends would be doing drugs.”']
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 3.3 — Change talk vs sustain talk' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Type', 'Change talk', 'Sustain talk'], rows: [
        ['Desire', '“I want to cut down on my drinking.”', '“I love how cocaine makes me feel.”'],
        ['Ability', '“I could cut back to 1 drink with dinner on weekends.”', '“I can manage my life just fine without giving up the drug.”'],
        ['Reasons', '“I’ll miss less time at work if I cut down.”', '“Getting high helps me feel energized.”'],
        ['Need', '“I have to cut down. My doctor told me that the amount I am drinking puts my health at risk.”', '“I need to get high to keep me going every day.”'],
        ['Commitment', '“I promise to cut back this weekend.”', '“I am going to keep snorting cocaine.”'],
        ['Activation', '“I am ready to do something about the drinking.”', '“I am not ready to give up the cocaine.”'],
        ['Taking steps', '“I only had one drink with dinner on Saturday.”', '“I am still snorting cocaine every day.”']
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Making use of change talk and sustain talk' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Recognize change talk, but <strong>don’t worry about differentiating the kinds</strong> during a session.',
        'Use reflective listening to reinforce change talk and help clients elaborate.',
        'Use DARN-CAT in conversations with clients.',
        'Recognize sustain talk and use MI strategies to lessen its impact.',
        'Expect both sides of ambivalence to be present.'
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'resistance', h: 'A New Look at Resistance', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Resistance was historically seen as a problem centered in the client — a pathological defense mechanism. MI now views it as <strong>a normal part of ambivalence and the client’s reaction to the counselor’s approach in the moment</strong>.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Resistance may show up as sustain talk favoring “no change.” How you respond can plant the client more firmly in the status quo or help the client move toward contemplating change. The client’s ambivalence plus <em>your arguments for change</em> can create discord.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Client sustain talk is often <strong>evoked by discord</strong> in the relationship. <strong>Resistance is a two-way street.</strong> If discord arises, change direction or listen more carefully — an opportunity to respond in a new, perhaps surprising, way without being confrontational.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'oars', h: 'Core Skills of MI: OARS — Open Questions and Affirming', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: '<strong>OARS</strong>: asking <strong>O</strong>pen questions, <strong>A</strong>ffirming, <strong>R</strong>eflective listening, <strong>S</strong>ummarizing. Used throughout your work, they bring greater success in engaging clients and less discord.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Asking open questions' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Open questions invite clients to reflect before answering and to elaborate; they facilitate dialog, let the client do most of the talking and keep the conversation moving. Closed questions evoke yes/no or short answers and can make clients feel they must give the “right” answer. A statement beginning <strong>“Tell me about…”</strong> or “Tell me more about…” works as an open question.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Closed question', 'Open question'], rows: [
        ['“So you are here because you are concerned about your use of alcohol, correct?”', '“What is it that brings you here today?”'],
        ['“How many children do you have?”', '“Tell me about your family.”'],
        ['“Do you agree that it would be a good idea for you to go through detoxification?”', '“What do you think about the possibility of going through detoxification?”'],
        ['“On a typical day, how much marijuana do you smoke?”', '“Tell me about your marijuana use on a typical day.”'],
        ['“Did your doctor tell you to quit smoking?”', '“What did your doctor tell you about the health risks of smoking?”'],
        ['“How has your drug use been this week compared with last week: more, less, or about the same?”', '“What has your drug use been like during the past week?”'],
        ['“Do you think you use amphetamines too often?”', '“In what ways are you concerned about your use of amphetamines?”'],
        ['“How long ago did you have your last drink?”', '“Tell me about the last time you drank.”'],
        ['“Are you sure that your probation officer told you that it’s only cocaine he is concerned about in your urine screens?”', '“Tell me more about the conditions of your probation.”'],
        ['“When do you plan to quit drinking?”', '“What do you think you want to do about your drinking?”']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Closed questions are sometimes needed (e.g., screening or assessment), but open questions often get the same information while enhancing engagement. Avoid the <strong>question-and-answer trap</strong>. Balance questions and reflections: <strong>ask one open question, then follow it with two or more reflective listening responses</strong>.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Affirming' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Affirming expresses genuine appreciation and positive regard; it supports and promotes <strong>self-efficacy</strong> and boosts confidence. It says, “I see you, what you say matters, and I want to understand what you think and feel.” Using affirmations consistently predicts positive client outcomes.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Emphasize strengths, past successes and efforts to take steps, however small.',
        'Don’t confuse affirming with <strong>praise</strong>, which can be a roadblock to listening.',
        'Frame affirmations with <strong>“you,” not “I”</strong> — not “I am proud of you” but “You have worked really hard to get to where you are now in your life.”',
        'Examples: “You took a big step in coming here today.” “You got discouraged last week but kept going to your AA meetings. You are persistent.” “Although things didn’t turn out the way you hoped, you tried really hard, and that means a lot.” “That’s a good idea for how you can avoid situations where you might be tempted to drink.”'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'People differ culturally and personally in how they receive affirmations. Watch verbal and nonverbal cues and check with an open question: “How was that for you to hear?” Strategies that account for these differences (Rosengren, 2018):' },
      { t: 'ul', items: ['Focus on specific behaviors.', 'Avoid using “I.”', 'Emphasize descriptions instead of evaluations.', 'Emphasize positive developments instead of continuing problems.', 'Affirm interesting qualities and strengths.', 'Hold an awareness of strengths, not deficits, as you formulate affirmations.'] }
    ] },
    { id: 'reflective', h: 'Reflective Listening', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Reflective listening is <strong>the key component of expressing empathy</strong> and fundamental to MI. It communicates respect and acceptance; establishes trust and invites clients to explore their own perceptions, values and feelings; encourages a nonjudgmental, collaborative relationship; and lets you be supportive <strong>without agreeing</strong> with specific statements.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'It is both an expression of empathy and a way to <strong>selectively reinforce change talk</strong>. Counselor empathy predicts better substance use outcomes. Your attitude is acceptance, not necessarily approval or agreement. Consider ethnic and cultural differences in how verbal and nonverbal communication is interpreted.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Expert comment (Cheryl Grills): empathy with African American clients', x: 'Be a genuine person, not just a counselor. Clients may ask about you as a person — “Are you Christian?” “Where are you from?” “Who are your folks?” “Are you married?” — to locate you in the world. These are reasonable questions that build a real relationship; in a democratic partnership clients have a right, and sometimes a cultural expectation, to know about the helper. Many African Americans are very spiritual; working with this (“I feel you”) can deepen the empathic connection beyond words.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Expert comment (Ray Daw): empathy with American Indian/Native American clients', x: 'For many traditional groups, empathy begins with the introduction. The counselor is expected to know and practice culturally accepted norms: at the first meeting, give your name, clan relationship or ethnic origin, and place of origin. Keep physical contact minimal — a brief handshake that may be no more than a soft touch of the palms.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Reflective listening is <strong>not just being quiet</strong>. You form a <strong>mental hypothesis</strong> about the underlying meaning or feeling and reflect back your best guess. Gordon (1970) called this “active listening” and named 12 responses that are not listening and can derail a conversation.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 3.5 — Gordon’s 12 roadblocks to active listening' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Roadblock', 'Why it blocks'], rows: [
        ['1. Ordering, directing, commanding', 'Direction given with a voice of authority; communicates that the speaker is the expert.'],
        ['2. Warning, cautioning, threatening', 'Overt or covert threat of consequences — “If you don’t stop drinking, you are going to die.”'],
        ['3. Giving advice or solutions prematurely or unsolicited', 'Recommends a course of action from your own experience — “What I would do is…”'],
        ['4. Persuading with logic, arguing, lecturing', 'Assumes the client hasn’t reasoned it through; trying to prove your position most likely makes the client take the opposite one.'],
        ['5. Moralizing, preaching, telling what they should do', '“Should” and “ought” convey negative judgment.'],
        ['6. Judging, criticizing, disagreeing, blaming', 'Implies something is wrong with the client; even simple disagreement may feel critical.'],
        ['7. Agreeing, approving, praising', 'An obstacle if it sanctions whatever the client said, or is too frequent or general (“great job”).'],
        ['8. Shaming, ridiculing, labeling', 'Express disapproval; damage self-esteem and the alliance.'],
        ['9. Interpreting or analyzing', 'Imposes hidden meaning; implies you know the “real” problem and puts you one-up.'],
        ['10. Reassuring, sympathizing, consoling', 'Sympathy is not empathy; reassurance interrupts communication and careful listening.'],
        ['11. Questioning or probing', 'Not the same as listening; intensive questioning can feel like interrogation.'],
        ['12. Withdrawing, distracting, humoring, changing the subject', 'Sometimes helpful, but can distract and disrupt communication.']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'In any of these 12, <strong>you are talking, not listening</strong>; they shift the focus from client to counselor.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 3.6 — Types of reflective listening responses' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Type', 'Client statement', 'Counselor response', 'Purpose', 'Special considerations'], rows: [
        ['Simple: Repeat', '“My wife is nagging me about my drinking.”', '“Your wife is nagging you about your drinking.”', 'Builds rapport. Expresses empathy.', 'Avoid mimicking.'],
        ['Simple: Rephrase', '“My wife is nagging me about my drinking.”', '“Your wife is pressuring you about your drinking.”', 'Expresses empathy. Highlights selected meaning or feeling.', 'Moves the conversation along, but more slowly than complex reflections.'],
        ['Complex: Feeling', '“I’d like to quit smoking marijuana so that the second-hand pot smoke won’t worsen my daughter’s asthma.”', '“You’re afraid that your daughter’s asthma will get worse if you continue smoking marijuana.”', 'Highlights selected feeling. Highlights discrepancy between values and behavior.', 'Selectively reinforce change talk; avoid reinforcing sustain talk.'],
        ['Complex: Meaning', '“I’d like to quit … because I read that second-hand pot smoke can make asthma worse and I don’t want that to happen to my daughter.”', '“You want to protect your daughter from the possibility that her asthma will get worse if you continue smoking marijuana.”', 'Highlights selected meaning. Highlights discrepancy.', 'Selectively reinforce change talk; avoid reinforcing sustain talk.'],
        ['Complex: Double-sided', '“I know I should give up drinking, but I can’t imagine life without it.”', '“Giving up drinking would be hard, <strong>and</strong> you recognize that it’s time to stop.”', 'Resolves ambivalence. Acknowledges sustain talk and emphasizes change talk.', 'Join with “and.” Start with the sustain talk reflection; <strong>end with change talk</strong>.'],
        ['Complex: Amplified', '“I think my cocaine use is just not a problem for me.”', '“There are absolutely no negative consequences of using cocaine.”', 'Intensifies sustain talk to evoke change talk.', 'Use sparingly. Avoid getting stuck in sustain talk.']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: '<strong>Simple</strong> reflections repeat or paraphrase and show genuine interest; <strong>complex</strong> reflections use different words for the underlying meaning or feeling and invite deeper self-exploration. The key to accurate empathy is shifting from expert-who-advises to <strong>supporting the client’s autonomy and expertise</strong>.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Forming a complex reflection' },
      { t: 'ol', items: [
        'Consider possible meanings — “I drink because I am lonely” might mean “I lost my spouse,” “It is hard for me to make friends,” or “I can’t think of anything to say when I am with my family.”',
        'Consider the larger conversational context (has the client noted not having much of a social life?).',
        'Make your best guess about the meaning.',
        'Offer a reflection — “You drink because it is hard for you to make friends.”',
        'Wait for the response. If the client keeps talking and expands, you are on target.',
        'Be open to being wrong; use feedback to form another hypothesis.'
      ] },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Reflection-to-question ratio', x: 'Follow an open question with <strong>at least one reflection — preferably two or three</strong> — before asking another question. A higher ratio of reflections to questions consistently predicts positive client outcomes.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'summarizing', h: 'Summarizing', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Summarizing is a form of reflective listening that distills the essence of several client statements. It is not a mere collection: you <strong>intentionally select</strong> meaningful statements to paint a fuller picture than single reflections.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Type', 'What it does'], rows: [
        ['Collecting', 'Recalls a series of related client statements, creating a narrative to reflect on.'],
        ['Linking', 'Reflects a client statement and links it to an earlier statement.'],
        ['Transitional', 'Wraps up a conversation or task; moves the client along the change process.'],
        ['Ambivalence', 'Gathers sustain talk and change talk from a session; acknowledges sustain talk but <strong>reinforces and highlights change talk</strong>.'],
        ['Recapitulation', 'Gathers all the change talk of many conversations; useful in the transition from one stage to the next when making a change plan.']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'End a summary by asking whether you <strong>left anything out</strong>. Summaries reinforce movement toward change: clients hear their change talk <strong>once</strong> when they say it, <strong>twice</strong> when you reflect it, and <strong>again</strong> when you summarize.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'engaging', h: 'Four Processes of MI: Engaging', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI moved from phases to <strong>overlapping processes</strong> — a shift from a linear, rigid model to a circular, fluid one within the counseling relationship. The four processes integrate the four principles of earlier versions.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Engaging is the first step in all counseling; techniques fail without a strong working relationship. Miller and Rollnick define engaging as “the process of establishing a mutually trusting and respectful helping relationship.” This ability is linked to positive outcomes such as reduced drinking.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Opening strategies' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Ask open questions instead of closed questions.',
        'Offer affirmations of self-efficacy, hope and confidence in the client’s ability to change.',
        'Emphasize reflective listening.',
        'Summarize to show you are listening and genuinely interested.',
        'Determine the client’s readiness to change or SOC stage.',
        'Avoid prematurely focusing on taking action.',
        'Don’t identify treatment goals until you have sufficiently explored readiness; then address ambivalence.'
      ] },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Dialog: engaging Jerry with OARS', x: 'Counselor: “Jerry, thanks for coming in. (Affirmation) What brings you here today?” (Open question) — Client: “My wife thinks I drink too much…” — Counselor reflects the wife’s concerns, then asks “What concerns you about it?” — Client: “I’m not sure… but I do wonder sometimes if I’m drinking too much.” — Counselor: “Too much for…?” (open sentence to complete) — The client describes awful mornings, poor concentration and memory, and arguments with his wife. Counselor: “You don’t think you’re that bad off, but you do wonder if maybe you’re overdoing it and hurting yourself and your relationship with your wife.” Counselor: “It takes courage to come talk to a stranger about something that’s scary to talk about.” (Affirmation) It ends with a <strong>summary</strong> and “Did I miss anything?”' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Avoiding traps' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Trap', 'What happens', 'How to avoid'], rows: [
        ['Expert', 'Acting as if you must have all the answers — contrary to MI spirit. Both of you have expertise.', 'Refrain from the <strong>righting reflex</strong> (the impulse to jump in and direct the client toward a change), which produces sustain talk and discord. Don’t argue: if you try to prove a point, the client takes the opposite side.'],
        ['Labeling', 'Labels like “alcoholic” or “addict” evoke shame; there is no evidence forcing a label helps, and it usually evokes discord.', 'Don’t argue about labels. (When Jerry’s counselor instead proposed an assessment to “determine whether or not you are,” Jerry replied that counseling would not help him.)'],
        ['Question-and-answer', 'Focus on information gathering feels like interrogation; closed Q&A puts you in the expert role and makes the client passive.', 'Ask open questions and follow them with reflections.'],
        ['Premature focus', 'Focusing on your agenda (e.g., inpatient admission) before the client is ready or before an alliance exists produces discord.', 'Match your approach to the client’s readiness.'],
        ['Blaming', 'Clients arrive guarded, expecting judgment about who is to blame.', 'Immediately reassure them that you are not interested in blaming anyone; your role is to listen.']
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'focusing', h: 'Focusing', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Once engaged, find a direction: with the client, develop a <strong>mutually agreed-on agenda</strong> that promotes change, then identify a <strong>specific target behavior</strong>. Without a clear focus, change conversations are unwieldy and unproductive.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Deciding on an agenda' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Direction is influenced by the client, the counselor and the setting. Clients may be mandated and not see substance use as a problem, or bring multiple issues (child care, relational, financial, legal). Then use <strong>agenda mapping</strong>.' },
      { t: 'p', x: '<strong>Exhibit 3.7 (sample agenda map)</strong>: a page of shapes, each holding one concern — Finances, Family Relationships, Drinking, Legal Problems, Medical Issues, Depression, Sleep Problems — with some shapes left blank.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Have an empty agenda map handy, or draw <strong>8 to 10 empty circles</strong> on paper.',
        'Introduce it: “I know you were referred here to address [drinking], but you may have other concerns… That way, we’ll have a map… How does that sound?”',
        'Write one concern per circle; <strong>leave two or three blank</strong>. If you suggest a topic, ask permission and leave the choice to the client.',
        'Ask which concern is most pressing: “(Summarize) Where would you like to start?”',
        'Leave time to guide the client back to substance use if it hasn’t been discussed.',
        'Keep the map as a visual record; refer back, add and delete topics.',
        'Use OARS throughout.'
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Identifying a target behavior' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Change talk links to a specific behavior change target — <strong>you can’t evoke change talk until you identify a target behavior</strong>.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Dialog: Marla', x: 'Counselor asks for Marla’s specific concerns about drinking. She describes drinking until she falls asleep, hangovers, lateness and a warning from her supervisor. Counselor reflects, then asks, “What do you think you’d like to change about the drinking?” Marla: “I think I need to stop drinking completely for a while.” Counselor <strong>asks permission</strong> to put “stop drinking for a while” on the map. Narrowing from drinking in general to a target behavior moves the work into <strong>evoking</strong>.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'evoking', h: 'Evoking Change Talk', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Evoking elicits client motivations so that <strong>clients, not counselors, argue for change</strong>. It is <strong>the core of MI</strong> and differentiates it from other methods. Your task is to help clients recognize that substance use may be contributing to their distress and that they have a choice. A sign of decreasing ambivalence is that clients start to express change talk.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'The first step is to ask open questions. <strong>DARN</strong> questions help you generate them.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 3.8 — Open questions to evoke change talk' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['DARN', 'Sample open questions'], rows: [
        ['Desire', '“How would you like for things to change?” “What don’t you like about how things are now?” “How do you want your life to be different a year from now?” “What are you looking for from this program?”'],
        ['Ability', '“If you decided to quit drinking, how could you do it?” “What encourages you that you could change if you decided to?” “Of the different options you’ve considered, what seems most possible?”'],
        ['Reasons', '“What are some of the reasons you have for making this change?” “What’s the downside of the way things are now?” “What might be the three best reasons for ___?”'],
        ['Need', '“What needs to happen?” “How important is it for you to ___?” “How serious or urgent does this feel to you?” “What do you think has to change?”']
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Other strategies for evoking change talk' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Eliciting importance</strong> — ask a “Need” question, or use the <strong>Importance Ruler</strong> (Exhibit 3.9).',
        '<strong>Exploring extremes</strong> — “What concerns you the most about [using cocaine]?”',
        '<strong>Looking back</strong> — compare before substance problems with now: “What was it like before you started using heroin?”',
        '<strong>Looking forward</strong> — “If you decided to [quit smoking], how do you think your life would be different a month, a year, or 5 years from now?”'
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 3.9 — The Importance Ruler (0 = not important, 10 = extremely important)' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Initial: “On a scale of 0 to 10, how important is it for you to change [target behavior] if you decided to?”',
        'Follow-up 1: “How are you at a [number] instead of a <strong>[lower number]</strong>?” A lower number invites reflection on how the client is already considering change (e.g., 6 not 3 → “drinking causes more problems in my life now”). A <strong>higher</strong> number (6 not 9) will likely evoke sustain talk (“I am just not ready to quit right this second”) — still useful information.',
        'Follow-up 2: “What would help move from a [number] to a [slightly higher number]?” — invites reasons to increase readiness.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Reinforce change talk by reflecting it, nodding, approving expressions and affirmations. Ask for elaboration, examples or details — <strong>“What else…?”</strong> questions invite elaboration. The amount of change talk versus sustain talk is linked to behavior change and outcomes.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'sustain-talk', h: 'Responding to Change Talk and Sustain Talk', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Don’t argue with sustain talk or try to persuade the client to the change side. Acknowledge it without getting stuck in it. All of these strategies are delivered <strong>in the spirit of MI</strong>:' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Strategy', 'How', 'Example'], rows: [
        ['Simple reflection', 'Acknowledge sustain talk; validates and sometimes elicits change talk. Let the client respond.', 'C: “I don’t plan to quit drinking anytime soon.” — “You don’t think that abstinence would work for you right now.”'],
        ['Amplified reflection', 'Reflect accurately but with emphasis (no sarcasm); overstating can nudge the client to the other side.', 'C: “I can’t quit smoking pot. All my friends smoke pot.” — “So you really can’t quit because you’d be too different from your friends.”'],
        ['Double-sided reflection', 'Acknowledge sustain talk, pair it with change talk (same or earlier statement), joined by “and,” change talk second.', 'C: “I know I should quit smoking now that I am pregnant. But I tried cold turkey before…” — “You’re worried that you won’t be able to quit all at once, and you want your baby to be born healthy.”'],
        ['Agreement with a twist', 'Agree, with a slight twist or change of direction, without emphasis or sarcasm.', 'C: “I can’t imagine what I would do if I stopped drinking. It’s part of who I am.” — “You just wouldn’t be you without drinking. You have to keep drinking no matter how it affects your health.”'],
        ['Reframing', 'Acknowledge the experience, suggest an alternative meaning; refocuses from sustain talk to change talk.', 'C: “My husband always nags me about my drinking and calls me an alcoholic.” — “Although your husband expresses it in a way that frustrates you, he really cares and is concerned about the drinking.”'],
        ['Shift in focus', 'Defuse discord by shifting the conversational focus.', 'C: “You think I’m an alcoholic, don’t you?” — “Labels aren’t important to me. What I care about is how to best help you.”'],
        ['Emphasis on personal autonomy', 'People have choices, even if all have a downside; use a neutral, nonjudgmental tone — a dismissive tone provokes strong reactions.', 'C: “I am really not interested in giving up drinking completely.” — “It’s really up to you. No one can make that decision for you.”']
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'discrepancy', h: 'Developing Discrepancy: A Values Conversation', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Developing discrepancy was one of the original four principles; it is now <strong>folded into evoking</strong>. When clients recognize discrepancies between behavior and their values, goals and hopes, motivation increases. The focus is on <strong>intrinsic motivation</strong> — MI doesn’t work if you focus only on conflict with <em>external</em> pressure (family, employer, court).' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Have a values conversation (good health, family relationships, being responsible in the community, avoiding another hospitalization, staying out of jail), then highlight the conflict the client feels between substance use and those values. Client experience of discrepancy relates to better outcomes.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'This can raise guilt or shame — frame it with acceptance, compassion and affirmation. <strong>The paradox of acceptance</strong>: it helps people tolerate more discrepancy and propels them toward change. But <strong>too much discrepancy may overwhelm</strong> the client into thinking change is impossible.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Expert comment (Cheryl Grills): the Columbo approach', x: 'From Kanfer & Schefft (1988); useful with a client in <strong>Precontemplation</strong> who needs to be in charge of the conversation. Like the TV detective, the counselor is puzzled because the clues don’t add up: “Hmm. Help me figure this out. You’ve told me that keeping custody of your daughter and being a good parent are the most important things to you now. How does your heroin use fit in with that?” Expressing confusion lets the <strong>client</strong> grapple with and resolve the discrepancy, reinforcing that the client is the expert. If the counselor resolves it instead, the counselor risks the wrong interpretation, rushing to conclusions and making the client passive.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Other ways to facilitate discrepancy' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Personalized feedback</strong> (Chapter 2).',
        '<strong>Identifying personal values — Values Card Sort</strong>: print values on cards (e.g., “Achievement—to have important accomplishments”); client sorts them into piles by importance; picks <strong>up to 10</strong> from the most important pile; talk about each using OARS; reinforce statements of discrepancy between those values and substance use.',
        '<strong>Providing information with Elicit-Provide-Elicit (EPE)</strong> — see below.',
        '<strong>Exploring others’ concerns</strong> — not external pressure, but the impact of use on people the client is emotionally connected to, with genuine curiosity and a bit of confusion.'
      ] },
      { t: 'table', head: ['EPE step', 'How'], rows: [
        ['Elicit readiness or interest', 'Ask permission — “Would it be okay if I shared some information with you about the health risks of using heroin?” Don’t assume they lack the knowledge; ask what they know and want to know.'],
        ['Provide information neutrally', 'Without judgment; prioritize what they want to know; fill gaps; small chunks — too much overwhelms; invite questions.'],
        ['Elicit understanding', 'Don’t assume their reaction: “So, what do you make of this information?” Allow time; reflect, emphasizing change talk.']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'EPE also works as a respectful way to give advice about change strategies during planning.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Example: exploring a husband’s concern', x: 'Counselor: “What do you think concerns him?” Client: “He worries about everything… I drove a block home after a party. He shouldn’t worry so much.” (Sustain talk) Counselor: “He’s worried that you could crash and hurt yourself or someone else or get arrested… But you think his concern is overblown.” (Complex reflection) Client: “I can see he may have a point. I really shouldn’t drive after drinking.” (Change talk) — <strong>reflecting sustain talk elicited change talk</strong>.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'confidence', h: 'Hope, Confidence and Readiness', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Many clients find it hard to believe they can change. Improving self-efficacy requires eliciting confidence, hope and optimism that change is possible in general and that <em>this</em> client can change. <strong>“Ability” change talk is one of the most consistent predictors</strong> of positive behavior change. Without belief that change is possible, desire plus hopelessness leads to continued sustain talk.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Counselor note: self-efficacy', x: 'Self-efficacy is a person’s confidence in his or her ability to change a behavior. MI enhances self-efficacy and is linked with treatment completion, lower substance use at end of treatment, greater desire to quit cannabis, and reduced risky sexual behavior in people with HIV. <strong>You must also believe</strong> in clients’ capacity to reach their goals.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Evoking confidence talk' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Confidence Ruler (Exhibit 3.10)</strong>: “On a scale of 0 to 10, how confident are you that you could [stop drinking] if you decided to?” Then “How are you at [X] instead of [lower number]?” (a higher number may discourage and elicit sustain talk) and “What would help you get from [X] to [<strong>slightly</strong> higher number]?” — don’t jump much higher; it can overwhelm and lower confidence.',
        '<strong>Open questions about strengths</strong>, followed by reflections: “Knowing yourself as well as you do, how do you think you could…?” “How have you made difficult changes in the past?” “How could you apply what you learned then to this situation?” “What gives you confidence that you could…?”'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Also enhance hope by: exploring strengths and how to apply them; giving information via EPE about treatment efficacy; discussing what did and didn’t work in previous treatment; describing how people in similar situations changed (other clients as role models); offering cognitive tools such as “One day at a time” or “Keep it simple”; and educating about the biology of addiction to alleviate shame and instill hope.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Recognizing signs of readiness to change' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Engaging, focusing and evoking evoke <strong>DARN</strong> change talk; moving toward action, your task becomes evoking and responding to <strong>CAT</strong> change talk. Signs of readiness:' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Sign', 'What you observe'], rows: [
        ['Increased change talk', 'Commitment and activation talk begin; optimism and intention to change.'],
        ['Decreased sustain talk', 'Change talk overtakes sustain talk.'],
        ['Resolve', 'Client seems more relaxed, talks less about the problem, may express resolution.'],
        ['Questions about change', 'Asks what to do, how people change — “What do people do to get off pain pills?”'],
        ['Envisioning', 'Talks about life after change, anticipates difficulties or advantages — imagining things different, not necessarily how to get there.'],
        ['Taking steps', 'Experiments with small steps (an AA meeting, a few days without drinking, a self-help book). Affirm them.']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'When you see these signs, offer a <strong>recapitulation summary</strong> (restate change talk, minimize reflections of sustain talk), then transition to <strong>key questions</strong>: “What do you think you will do about your drinking?” “After reviewing the situation, what’s the next step for you?” “Where do you go from here?”' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Change talk in response (“I intend to stop using heroin”) → move to planning. Sustain talk (“It would be too hard for me to quit right now”) → <strong>go back to evoking</strong>. Change is not linear; the ambivalence may be about giving up the behavior or about confidence.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'planning', h: 'Planning and Strengthening Commitment', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Help the client develop a change plan that is <strong>acceptable, accessible and appropriate</strong>. Clients may already know what worked before — then simply reinforce their plan. Don’t assume everyone needs a structured method; many people recover from SUDs without formal assistance. Keep using OARS to move from <em>why</em> and <em>what</em> to <em>how</em>. A change plan is like a treatment plan but <strong>broader</strong>, and <strong>the client is the driver</strong>.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Identifying a change goal' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'A key question may yield a clear goal (“cut back to two drinks a day on weekends”) — then plan specific steps. If vague (“I really need to do something about my drinking”), first help clarify the goal.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Dialog: clarifying the goal', x: 'Client tried cutting back to one drink a day, lasted 2 days, ended up at the bar. Counselor: “You made a good-faith effort to control the drinking and learned a lot from that experiment.” (Affirmation) “You now think that cutting back is probably not a good strategy for you.” (Reflection) Client: “It’s time to quit. But I’m not sure I can do that on my own.” The counselor reflects readiness <em>and</em> the wish for help, hearing low confidence.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Developing a change plan' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Start from the client’s goal; use OARS and listen for <strong>CAT</strong> change talk, noting the shift from general to plan-specific change talk.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Confirm the change goal</strong> — which behavior, and cut back or abstain. It may change (cutting back on cannabis may prove unworkable → abstain).',
        '<strong>Elicit the client’s ideas first</strong> — e.g., AA or SMART Recovery, naltrexone from a primary care provider (reduces craving and the pleasurable effects of alcohol), intensive outpatient, or a combination. Explore pros and cons.',
        '<strong>Offer a menu of options</strong> using EPE.',
        '<strong>Summarize the change plan</strong> and reinforce CAT change talk.',
        '<strong>Explore obstacles</strong> — anticipate setbacks before implementation; then reevaluate and tweak the plan.'
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Strengthening commitment to change' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Planning is the beginning; clients must commit and act. Commitment change talk is associated with positive AUD outcomes, and counselor efforts to elicit commitment are associated with reduced drinking and more abstinence. People usually express <strong>intention</strong> before firm <strong>commitment</strong>: “What are you willing to do this week?” Then elicit commitment: “What would help you strengthen your commitment to [getting that naltrexone prescription]?”' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Explore ambivalence about goals or plan elements.',
        'Reinforce CAT change talk through reflective listening.',
        'Invite clients to state their commitment to significant others.',
        'Ask clients to self-monitor (e.g., a drinking log).',
        'With consent, explore whether supportive significant others can help (medication adherence, getting to AA).'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'The change plan lends itself to CBT and MET (e.g., monitoring thoughts and feelings in high-risk situations; see Chapter 7). Whatever method, keep the spirit of MI.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'benefits', h: 'Benefits of MI in Treating SUDs', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'MI research doubled about every 3 years from 1999 to 2013. Overall, MI has <strong>small to strong, significant effects</strong> on substance use outcomes compared with no treatment, and is <strong>as effective as other counseling approaches</strong>. Strong support for MI and MI/MET in <strong>alcohol, tobacco and cannabis</strong>; some support for cocaine and combined illicit drug use. Outcomes: reduced use, fewer alcohol-related problems, better engagement and retention. Effective with adolescents, young adults, college students, adults and pregnant women.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Counselor adherence to MI skills matters: open questions, simple and complex reflections and affirmations are associated with change talk (though they can also elicit sustain talk while exploring ambivalence). The <strong>amount and strength of change talk versus sustain talk</strong> are key components associated with behavior change.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Cost effectiveness</strong> — deliverable in brief interventions like SBIRT and FRAMES; including significant others is also cost effective.',
        '<strong>Ease of use</strong> — primary care, emergency departments, behavioral health, criminal justice and social service settings.',
        '<strong>Applicability</strong> to diverse health and behavioral health problems.',
        '<strong>Effectiveness</strong> across real-life clinical settings.',
        '<strong>Complements other treatments</strong> such as CBT; enhances engagement, retention and adherence.',
        '<strong>Ease of adoption</strong> by primary care and behavioral health professionals, peer providers, criminal justice personnel and others.',
        '<strong>Mobilizes client resources</strong> — consistent with the model of self-managing chronic illnesses like diabetes and heart disease.',
        '<strong>Broad dissemination</strong> in the United States and internationally.'
      ] },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Conclusion', x: 'MI is a <strong>directed, person-centered counseling style</strong>. Delivered in the spirit of MI, OARS enhances motivation and readiness. Counselor empathy, shown through reflective listening and evoking change talk, is associated with positive outcomes. MI is compatible with other models, including CBT and the SOC.' }
    ] }
  ],
  highYield: [
    'MI = a person-centered counseling style for resolving ambivalence; <strong>more directive</strong> than purely Rogerian counseling because it guides toward a client-driven change goal.',
    'Spirit of MI = <strong>PACE</strong>: Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, Evocation. Without it, MI techniques are ineffective.',
    'Acceptance has four parts: absolute worth, accurate empathy, autonomy support, affirmation.',
    '“Motivation for change is evoked from, not given to, the client.” “It is not a change goal until the client says so.”',
    'Updated MI: four <strong>processes</strong> (engaging, focusing, evoking, planning) replace the four principles (express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance, support self-efficacy).',
    '“Self-motivating statements” are now called <strong>change talk</strong>; “rolling with resistance” is now strategies to lessen sustain talk and discord.',
    'Decisional balance in updated MI is neutral and may <strong>increase</strong> ambivalence in contemplators.',
    'Change talk = <strong>DARN-CAT</strong>: Desire, Ability, Reasons, Need (preparatory) / Commitment, Activation, Taking steps (mobilizing).',
    'More sustain talk → poorer outcomes; more and stronger change talk → better outcomes.',
    'Resistance is not a client trait: it is sustain talk plus discord — <strong>a two-way street</strong>.',
    'OARS: Open questions, Affirming, Reflective listening, Summarizing.',
    'Ask one open question, then give <strong>2–3 reflections</strong>; a higher reflection-to-question ratio predicts better outcomes.',
    'Affirm with “you,” not “I”; affirming is not praise.',
    'Gordon’s <strong>12 roadblocks</strong>: when you use them you are talking, not listening.',
    'Double-sided reflection: sustain talk first, <strong>“and”</strong>, change talk last.',
    'Amplified reflection: overstate sustain talk without sarcasm; use sparingly.',
    'Five summaries: collecting, linking, transitional, ambivalence, recapitulation (all change talk, at transition to planning).',
    'Traps: expert (righting reflex), labeling, question-and-answer, premature focus, blaming.',
    'Agenda map: 8–10 circles, leave 2–3 blank, client picks where to start.',
    'You can’t evoke change talk until you identify a <strong>target behavior</strong>.',
    'Rulers: ask why they are at X <strong>instead of a lower number</strong>; a higher number evokes sustain talk.',
    'Evoking is the core of MI; developing discrepancy is now part of evoking and must focus on <strong>intrinsic</strong> values.',
    'EPE: Elicit (permission/what they know) → Provide (neutral, small chunks) → Elicit (their understanding).',
    '“Ability” change talk is one of the most consistent predictors of change.',
    'Signs of readiness → recapitulation summary → key questions; sustain talk in reply means go back to evoking.'
  ],
  cards: [
    { id: 'c01', tag: 'Intro', q: 'What four assumptions underlie MI as a counseling style?', a: 'Ambivalence is normal and a key barrier; it can be resolved by exploring intrinsic motivations and values; the alliance is a collaborative partnership of shared expertise; an empathic, supportive style creates conditions for change.' },
    { id: 'c02', tag: 'Intro', q: 'What are your two main goals as an MI counselor?', a: 'Express empathy, and elicit clients’ reasons for and commitment to changing substance use behaviors.' },
    { id: 'c03', tag: 'Intro', q: 'In which SOC stages is MI particularly helpful?', a: 'Precontemplation and Contemplation, when readiness is low — though it is useful throughout the cycle.' },
    { id: 'c04', tag: 'Spirit', q: 'What does PACE stand for?', a: 'Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, Evocation — the spirit of MI.' },
    { id: 'c05', tag: 'Spirit', q: 'Name the four components of acceptance.', a: 'Absolute worth, accurate empathy, autonomy support, affirmation.' },
    { id: 'c06', tag: 'Spirit', q: 'Does acceptance mean agreeing with the client?', a: 'No. It shows your intention to understand the client’s point of view and concerns, without necessarily agreeing.' },
    { id: 'c07', tag: 'Spirit', q: 'In the MI partnership, who drives the conversation?', a: 'The client. You are influential, but the client drives.' },
    { id: 'c08', tag: 'Spirit', q: 'What is evocation?', a: 'Eliciting and exploring the motivations, values, strengths and resources the client already has.' },
    { id: 'c09', tag: 'Person-centered', q: 'What are Rogers’ “critical conditions for change”?', a: 'A relationship in which the counselor is genuine and warm, expresses unconditional positive regard, and displays accurate empathy.' },
    { id: 'c10', tag: 'Person-centered', q: 'How does MI differ from purely person-centered counseling?', a: 'It is more directive: it guides the conversation toward a specific, client-driven change goal.' },
    { id: 'c11', tag: 'Person-centered', q: 'When does something become a change goal?', a: 'Only when the client says so.' },
    { id: 'c12', tag: 'What’s new', q: 'What were the four principles of the original MI?', a: 'Express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance, support self-efficacy.' },
    { id: 'c13', tag: 'What’s new', q: 'What are the four processes of updated MI, and the role of each?', a: 'Engaging (relational foundation), focusing (agenda and change goals), evoking (core skills toward a specific goal), planning (bridge to behavior change).' },
    { id: 'c14', tag: 'What’s new', q: 'How has decisional balancing changed in updated MI?', a: 'It is used neutrally to help clients decide without favoring a direction; it may help assess readiness but may increase ambivalence in clients who are contemplating change.' },
    { id: 'c15', tag: 'What’s new', q: 'What is the updated term for “self-motivating statements”?', a: 'Change talk.' },
    { id: 'c16', tag: 'Misconceptions', q: 'Is MI a counseling technique?', a: 'No — it is a style of being with people that uses specific clinical skills to foster motivation to change.' },
    { id: 'c17', tag: 'Misconceptions', q: 'Is personalized assessment feedback a necessary part of MI?', a: 'No. MI-style assessment feedback became MET; feedback can help clients low in readiness but is not required for MI.' },
    { id: 'c18', tag: 'Misconceptions', q: 'Can counselors motivate clients to change?', a: 'No. You cannot manufacture motivation that isn’t already there; MI is a partnership to discover the client’s own motivation.' },
    { id: 'c19', tag: 'Ambivalence', q: 'Define ambivalence as TIP 35 describes it in SUDs.', a: 'The tension between knowing the risks or needing to stop and continuing to use — feeling two ways about an important change. It is normal.' },
    { id: 'c20', tag: 'Ambivalence', q: 'Why should ambivalence not be treated as denial or resistance?', a: 'It is a normal part of change; interpreting it as denial evokes counselor–client discord, which is counterproductive.' },
    { id: 'c21', tag: 'Change talk', q: 'Define change talk and sustain talk.', a: 'Change talk: client statements favoring change. Sustain talk: client statements supporting not changing a health-risk behavior.' },
    { id: 'c22', tag: 'Change talk', q: 'Expand DARN-CAT.', a: 'Desire, Ability, Reasons, Need, Commitment, Activation, Taking steps.' },
    { id: 'c23', tag: 'Change talk', q: '“I’m ready to go to my first AA meeting.” Which kind of change talk?', a: 'Activation.' },
    { id: 'c24', tag: 'Change talk', q: '“I only had one drink with dinner on Saturday.” Which kind of change talk?', a: 'Taking steps.' },
    { id: 'c25', tag: 'Change talk', q: '“I need to get high to keep me going every day.” Change or sustain talk, and which type?', a: 'Sustain talk — Need.' },
    { id: 'c26', tag: 'Change talk', q: 'During a session, should you classify each change talk statement by type?', a: 'No — recognize change talk and reinforce it with reflections; don’t worry about differentiating kinds in session.' },
    { id: 'c27', tag: 'Resistance', q: 'How does updated MI view resistance?', a: 'As a normal part of ambivalence and the client’s reaction to the counselor’s approach in the moment — sustain talk arising from discord, not a client trait.', why: '“Resistance is a two-way street.”' },
    { id: 'c28', tag: 'Resistance', q: 'What should you do when discord arises?', a: 'Change direction or listen more carefully — respond in a new, perhaps surprising, non-confrontational way.' },
    { id: 'c29', tag: 'OARS', q: 'What does OARS stand for?', a: 'Open questions, Affirming, Reflective listening, Summarizing.' },
    { id: 'c30', tag: 'OARS', q: 'Convert to an open question: “How many children do you have?”', a: '“Tell me about your family.”' },
    { id: 'c31', tag: 'OARS', q: 'What ratio of questions to reflections does TIP 35 recommend?', a: 'One open question followed by at least one, preferably two or three, reflections.' },
    { id: 'c32', tag: 'OARS', q: 'Rewrite “I am proud of you” as an MI affirmation.', a: 'Use “you,” not “I”: e.g., “You have worked really hard to get to where you are now in your life.”' },
    { id: 'c33', tag: 'OARS', q: 'How can you check a client’s reaction to an affirmation?', a: 'Watch verbal and nonverbal cues and ask an open question: “How was that for you to hear?”' },
    { id: 'c34', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'What is the key component of expressing empathy?', a: 'Reflective listening.' },
    { id: 'c35', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'Who described the 12 roadblocks to active listening?', a: 'Gordon (1970).' },
    { id: 'c36', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'Why is reassuring or sympathizing a roadblock?', a: 'Sympathy is not empathy; reassurance interrupts communication and interferes with careful listening.' },
    { id: 'c37', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'How do you build a double-sided reflection?', a: 'Reflect sustain talk first, join with “and,” end with change talk.' },
    { id: 'c38', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'Client: “I think my cocaine use is just not a problem for me.” Give an amplified reflection.', a: '“There are absolutely no negative consequences of using cocaine.” Use sparingly, without sarcasm.' },
    { id: 'c39', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'What is the first step in forming a complex reflection?', a: 'Form a mental hypothesis about what the client is trying to say (consider possible meanings).' },
    { id: 'c40', tag: 'Reflections', q: 'How do you know your reflection is on target?', a: 'The client continues talking and expands on the initial statement.' },
    { id: 'c41', tag: 'Summaries', q: 'Which summary gathers all the change talk of many conversations?', a: 'Recapitulation summary — useful at the transition to making a change plan.' },
    { id: 'c42', tag: 'Summaries', q: 'What should an ambivalence summary do?', a: 'Gather sustain and change talk from the session, acknowledge sustain talk, but reinforce and highlight change talk.' },
    { id: 'c43', tag: 'Summaries', q: 'How should you end a summary?', a: 'Ask the client whether you left anything out.' },
    { id: 'c44', tag: 'Engaging', q: 'Define engaging (Miller & Rollnick).', a: '“The process of establishing a mutually trusting and respectful helping relationship.”' },
    { id: 'c45', tag: 'Traps', q: 'What is the righting reflex, and which trap does it feed?', a: 'The natural impulse to jump in and direct the client toward a specific change; it feeds the expert trap and produces sustain talk and discord.' },
    { id: 'c46', tag: 'Traps', q: 'A counselor admits a client and pushes inpatient treatment in the first minutes. Which trap?', a: 'Premature focus trap.' },
    { id: 'c47', tag: 'Traps', q: 'How do you avoid the blaming trap?', a: 'Immediately reassure clients that you are not interested in blaming anyone and your role is to listen.' },
    { id: 'c48', tag: 'Focusing', q: 'How do you set up an agenda map?', a: '8–10 empty circles; one concern per circle; leave 2–3 blank; ask permission to add topics; client chooses where to start; keep it as a visual record.' },
    { id: 'c49', tag: 'Focusing', q: 'Why must you identify a target behavior before evoking?', a: 'Change talk links to a specific behavior change target; you can’t evoke change talk without one.' },
    { id: 'c50', tag: 'Evoking', q: 'Importance Ruler: client says 6. What follow-up question evokes change talk?', a: '“How are you at a 6 instead of a 3?” (a lower number). Asking “instead of a 9” evokes sustain talk.' },
    { id: 'c51', tag: 'Evoking', q: 'Name four other strategies for evoking change talk besides DARN questions.', a: 'Eliciting importance (Importance Ruler), exploring extremes, looking back, looking forward.' },
    { id: 'c52', tag: 'Sustain talk', q: 'Client: “I can’t imagine what I would do if I stopped drinking. It’s part of who I am.” Name the strategy: “You just wouldn’t be you without drinking. You have to keep drinking no matter how it affects your health.”', a: 'Agreement with a twist.' },
    { id: 'c53', tag: 'Sustain talk', q: 'Client: “My husband always nags me and calls me an alcoholic.” Give a reframe.', a: '“Although your husband expresses it in a way that frustrates you, he really cares and is concerned about the drinking.”' },
    { id: 'c54', tag: 'Discrepancy', q: 'What is the Columbo approach?', a: 'The counselor plays a puzzled detective whose clues don’t add up, so the client explains and resolves the discrepancy — useful in Precontemplation.' },
    { id: 'c55', tag: 'Discrepancy', q: 'Why must discrepancy focus on intrinsic motivation?', a: 'MI doesn’t work if you focus only on conflict with external pressure (family, employer, court).' },
    { id: 'c56', tag: 'Discrepancy', q: 'What are the three steps of EPE?', a: 'Elicit readiness/interest (ask permission, what they know) → Provide information neutrally in small chunks → Elicit their understanding.' },
    { id: 'c57', tag: 'Confidence', q: 'Which type of change talk is one of the most consistent predictors of behavior change?', a: '“Ability” change talk.' },
    { id: 'c58', tag: 'Confidence', q: 'Confidence Ruler: why go only slightly higher in “What would help you get from X to…?”', a: 'Jumping to a much higher number can overwhelm clients and lower confidence.' },
    { id: 'c59', tag: 'Readiness', q: 'List the six signs of readiness to change.', a: 'Increased change talk, decreased sustain talk, resolve, questions about change, envisioning, taking steps.' },
    { id: 'c60', tag: 'Readiness', q: 'You ask a key question and the client answers, “It would be too hard to quit right now.” What next?', a: 'Go back to evoking; don’t jump into planning.' },
    { id: 'c61', tag: 'Planning', q: 'What three qualities should a change plan have, and who drives it?', a: 'Acceptable, accessible and appropriate; the client is the driver.' },
    { id: 'c62', tag: 'Planning', q: 'Name the five strategies for developing a change plan.', a: 'Confirm the change goal, elicit the client’s ideas, offer a menu of options (EPE), summarize the plan, explore obstacles.' },
    { id: 'c63', tag: 'Commitment', q: 'Name strategies to strengthen commitment.', a: 'Explore ambivalence about goals/plan; reinforce CAT talk; state commitment to significant others; self-monitor (drinking log); with consent, involve supportive others.' },
    { id: 'c64', tag: 'Evidence', q: 'For which substances is support for MI and MI/MET strongest?', a: 'Alcohol, tobacco and cannabis (some support for cocaine and combined illicit drug use).' }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { id: 'q01', q: 'A resident says MI is basically nondirective Rogerian therapy. The best correction is:', choices: ['MI rejects person-centered principles', 'MI has a strategic component guiding toward a specific behavioral change goal', 'MI is a comprehensive theory of change', 'MI requires personalized assessment feedback'], answer: 1, why: 'MI shares Rogerian principles but is more directive, with target behaviors and change goals.' },
    { id: 'q02', q: 'Which is NOT one of the four components of acceptance?', choices: ['Autonomy support', 'Absolute worth', 'Compassion', 'Accurate empathy'], answer: 2, why: 'Compassion is a separate PACE element; acceptance = absolute worth, accurate empathy, autonomy support, affirmation.' },
    { id: 'q03', q: 'In updated MI, what replaced the four principles as the basis of the approach?', choices: ['The four processes: engaging, focusing, evoking, planning', 'FRAMES', 'The Stages of Change', 'Phase I and Phase II'], answer: 0, why: 'The four processes replaced the principles and the Phase I/II stages.' },
    { id: 'q04', q: 'A client says, “I can manage my life just fine without giving up the drug.” This is:', choices: ['Change talk — Ability', 'Change talk — Activation', 'Sustain talk — Reasons', 'Sustain talk — Ability'], answer: 3, why: 'Exhibit 3.3 lists this as Ability sustain talk.' },
    { id: 'q05', q: '“I swear I will go to an AA meeting this year” is which kind of change talk?', choices: ['Commitment', 'Desire', 'Need', 'Activation'], answer: 0, why: 'A promise to change = Commitment.' },
    { id: 'q06', q: 'Updated MI understands client resistance as:', choices: ['A pathological defense mechanism', 'A stable personality trait', 'Sustain talk and discord arising in the relationship', 'Evidence the client is in Precontemplation'], answer: 2, why: 'Resistance is a two-way street — sustain talk often evoked by counselor–client discord.' },
    { id: 'q07', q: 'After asking one open question, MI recommends you:', choices: ['Ask a closed question to confirm', 'Offer two or three reflections before the next question', 'Give advice', 'Summarize immediately'], answer: 1, why: 'A higher reflection-to-question ratio predicts better outcomes.' },
    { id: 'q08', q: 'Which affirmation best follows TIP 35 guidance?', choices: ['“Great job!”', '“I am proud of you.”', '“You got discouraged last week but kept going to your AA meetings. You are persistent.”', '“I think you are doing better than most clients.”'], answer: 2, why: 'Specific, descriptive and framed with “you,” not “I”; general praise is a roadblock.' },
    { id: 'q09', q: '“If you don’t stop drinking, you are going to die” is which of Gordon’s roadblocks?', choices: ['Moralizing', 'Interpreting', 'Ordering', 'Warning or threatening'], answer: 3, why: 'It carries a threat of negative consequences.' },
    { id: 'q10', q: 'Client: “I know I should give up drinking, but I can’t imagine life without it.” The best double-sided reflection is:', choices: ['“You recognize it’s time to stop, but giving up drinking would be hard.”', '“Giving up drinking would be hard, and you recognize that it’s time to stop.”', '“You can’t imagine life without drinking.”', '“There’s no way you could ever stop.”'], answer: 1, why: 'Start with sustain talk, join with “and,” end with change talk.' },
    { id: 'q11', q: 'Which summary gathers the change talk of many conversations at the transition to making a change plan?', choices: ['Linking', 'Collecting', 'Transitional', 'Recapitulation'], answer: 3, why: 'The recapitulation summary.' },
    { id: 'q12', q: 'A client says, “But I don’t think I’m an alcoholic.” The counselor replies that an assessment will determine whether he is. Which trap is this?', choices: ['Labeling trap', 'Blaming trap', 'Question-and-answer trap', 'Premature focus trap'], answer: 0, why: 'Forcing a label evokes discord — the client then said counseling would not help.' },
    { id: 'q13', q: 'When creating an agenda map, you should:', choices: ['Fill every circle with the referral problem', 'Leave two or three circles blank', 'Decide the starting topic for the client', 'Discard it after the first session'], answer: 1, why: 'Blank circles let you add client concerns or ask permission to suggest a topic.' },
    { id: 'q14', q: 'On the Importance Ruler the client says 6. Which question is most likely to evoke change talk?', choices: ['“How are you at a 6 instead of a 9?”', '“Why not a 10?”', '“How are you at a 6 instead of a 3?”', '“Are you sure it’s a 6?”'], answer: 2, why: 'A lower number invites reflection on how the client is already considering change; a higher number evokes sustain talk.' },
    { id: 'q15', q: 'Client: “I can’t quit smoking pot. All my friends smoke pot.” Counselor: “So you really can’t quit because you’d be too different from your friends.” This is:', choices: ['Reframing', 'Shift in focus', 'Simple reflection', 'Amplified reflection'], answer: 3, why: 'It overstates the client’s point to nudge toward the other side of ambivalence.' },
    { id: 'q16', q: '“Hmm. Help me figure this out. Keeping custody of your daughter matters most to you. How does your heroin use fit in with that?” This illustrates:', choices: ['The Columbo approach', 'Agreement with a twist', 'The expert trap', 'A linking summary'], answer: 0, why: 'The puzzled-detective style lets the client resolve the discrepancy.' },
    { id: 'q17', q: 'The first step of Elicit-Provide-Elicit is to:', choices: ['Give all the health risks at once', 'Ask permission and what the client already knows', 'Ask how the client will change', 'Summarize the client’s ambivalence'], answer: 1, why: 'Elicit readiness or interest first; don’t assume lack of knowledge.' },
    { id: 'q18', q: 'Which type of change talk is one of the most consistent predictors of positive behavior change?', choices: ['Desire', 'Reasons', 'Need', 'Ability'], answer: 3, why: 'Confidence in ability to change predicts engagement in change.' },
    { id: 'q19', q: 'You ask a key question and the client replies with sustain talk. You should:', choices: ['Return to the evoking process', 'Proceed to a structured change plan', 'Point out the contradiction firmly', 'End the session'], answer: 0, why: 'Change is not linear; don’t jump into planning when sustain talk shows the client isn’t ready.' },
    { id: 'q20', q: 'Which statement about the change plan is correct?', choices: ['It is narrower than a treatment plan', 'The counselor drives it', 'It is broader than a treatment plan and the client drives it', 'Every client needs a structured method'], answer: 2, why: 'It is like a treatment plan but broader, with the client as driver; many recover without formal help.' }
  ],
  glossary: [
    { term: 'Spirit of MI', def: 'The collaborative way of being with clients that underlies MI skills: Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, Evocation (PACE).', alt: ['PACE', 'MI spirit'] },
    { term: 'Absolute worth', def: 'Component of acceptance: prizing the inherent worth and potential of the client.' },
    { term: 'Accurate empathy', def: 'Component of acceptance: active interest in and effort to understand the client’s internal perspective, shown through genuine curiosity and reflective listening.' },
    { term: 'Autonomy support', def: 'Component of acceptance: honoring the client’s right to and capacity for self-direction.' },
    { term: 'Evocation', def: 'PACE element: eliciting and exploring the motivations, values, strengths and resources the client already has.' },
    { term: 'Change talk', def: 'Client statements that favor change. Kinds: Desire, Ability, Reasons, Need, Commitment, Activation, Taking steps.', alt: ['DARN-CAT'] },
    { term: 'Sustain talk', def: 'Client statements that support not changing a health-risk behavior — the status quo side of ambivalence.' },
    { term: 'Discord', def: 'Tension in the counselor–client relationship that often evokes sustain talk; part of the updated view of resistance.' },
    { term: 'OARS', def: 'The core MI skills: Open questions, Affirming, Reflective listening, Summarizing.' },
    { term: 'Open question', def: 'A question (or “Tell me about…” statement) that invites the client to reflect and elaborate rather than give a brief answer.', alt: ['open questions'] },
    { term: 'Closed question', def: 'A question that evokes a yes/no or short answer.', alt: ['closed questions'] },
    { term: 'Affirming', def: 'Expressing genuine appreciation and positive regard for the client’s strengths and efforts, framed with “you”; supports self-efficacy. Not the same as praise.', alt: ['affirmations'] },
    { term: 'Reflective listening', def: 'Forming a hypothesis about the meaning or feeling behind a client statement and reflecting it back; the key component of expressing empathy.', alt: ['active listening'] },
    { term: 'Simple reflection', def: 'Repeating or rephrasing a client statement; builds rapport and expresses empathy.', alt: ['simple reflections'] },
    { term: 'Complex reflection', def: 'Using different words to reflect the underlying meaning or feeling of a statement; invites deeper self-exploration.', alt: ['complex reflections'] },
    { term: 'Double-sided reflection', def: 'A reflection that acknowledges sustain talk and then, joined by “and,” reflects change talk last.', alt: ['double-sided reflections'] },
    { term: 'Roadblocks to listening', def: 'Gordon’s 12 responses (e.g., ordering, warning, advising, arguing, praising, reassuring, probing) that shift focus to the counselor and derail listening.', alt: ['12 roadblocks'] },
    { term: 'Recapitulation summary', def: 'A summary gathering all the change talk of many conversations; used when moving toward a change plan and before key questions.' },
    { term: 'Ambivalence summary', def: 'A summary of the session’s sustain and change talk that acknowledges sustain talk but highlights change talk.' },
    { term: 'Four processes', def: 'The basis of updated MI: engaging, focusing, evoking and planning — overlapping, not linear.', alt: ['four processes of MI'] },
    { term: 'Engaging', def: 'MI process of establishing a mutually trusting and respectful helping relationship.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Focusing', def: 'MI process of agreeing on an agenda and a specific target behavior.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Evoking', def: 'The core MI process: eliciting the client’s own motivations so the client argues for change.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Planning', def: 'MI process of developing a change plan that is acceptable, accessible and appropriate; the bridge to behavior change.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Righting reflex', def: 'The natural impulse to jump into action and direct the client toward a specific change; produces sustain talk and discord.' },
    { term: 'Expert trap', def: 'Acting as though you must have all the answers, contrary to the collaboration of expertise.' },
    { term: 'Labeling trap', def: 'Pushing diagnoses or labels like “alcoholic,” which evokes shame and discord.' },
    { term: 'Question-and-answer trap', def: 'A pattern of closed questions and short answers that feels like interrogation and makes the client passive.' },
    { term: 'Premature focus trap', def: 'Focusing on your agenda for change before the client is ready or an alliance exists.' },
    { term: 'Blaming trap', def: 'Getting drawn into who is to blame; avoided by stating you are not interested in blaming anyone.' },
    { term: 'Agenda mapping', def: 'A visual way to list a client’s concerns (8–10 circles, some blank) and let the client choose the focus.', alt: ['agenda map'] },
    { term: 'Target behavior', def: 'The specific behavior the client is ready to discuss changing; change talk can’t be evoked without one.' },
    { term: 'Importance Ruler', def: 'A 0–10 scaling question on how important change is, followed by “Why X instead of a lower number?” and “What would move you higher?”' },
    { term: 'Confidence Ruler', def: 'A 0–10 scaling question on confidence to change, used to assess confidence and evoke confidence talk.' },
    { term: 'Agreement with a twist', def: 'Agreeing with the client but with a slight change of direction, without emphasis or sarcasm.', alt: ['agreements with a twist'] },
    { term: 'Reframing', def: 'Acknowledging the client’s experience while suggesting an alternative meaning, to shift from sustain talk toward change talk.' },
    { term: 'Columbo approach', def: 'A puzzled, detective-like style that lets the client grapple with and resolve discrepancies; useful in Precontemplation.' },
    { term: 'Values Card Sort', def: 'A tool where clients sort value cards by importance and discuss up to 10 most important, to surface discrepancy with substance use.' },
    { term: 'Elicit-Provide-Elicit', def: 'Giving information as a mutual exchange: elicit readiness and knowledge, provide information neutrally in small chunks, elicit understanding.', alt: ['EPE'] },
    { term: 'Confidence talk', def: 'Client statements expressing hope and confidence about the ability to change; evoked to build self-efficacy.' },
    { term: 'Key questions', def: 'Questions that help a ready client move to mobilizing, e.g., “What’s the next step for you?”' },
    { term: 'Change plan', def: 'A client-driven plan for how to change, broader than a treatment plan; acceptable, accessible and appropriate.' }
  ]
});
